3) What is REST API and its Architecture ?
      REST API, stands for Representational State Transfer Application Programming Interface
      - REST is stateless ( THe server doesnt remember previous requests from the client. Every request must contain all
        the info needed to process it.)

   Principles of REST API
   ----------------------
      - Client-server Architecture
      - Stateless
      - Can be cached
      - Opacque in terms of layers
      - Uniform interface (all the end points that are designed are uniform in nature)

   Web Services built following the REST architectural style are known as RESTFUL web services

   Benefits
   --------
      - Simplicity ( working on http protocals makes REST API simple to use and implement )
      - Scalability ( since they are stateless in nature means they can scale large number of requests )
      - Flexibility ( Data is not tied to resources or methods which allows for more flexibility in how data is returned )
      - Visibility ( Communication btw client and server is done using standard http methods )


4) http VS https
      1) Both of these are protocals that are used for transmitting infomation over the internet ( across the world wide web) .
      2) They operate based on a client-server model, where a client( web browser ) sends a req to the server hosting a website.
      3) Both protocols use similar methods to perform actions on the web server as well as status codes
      4) HTTP and HTTPS are both stateless protocols, meaning they dont inherently remember anything about the previous web session
      5) Both HTTP and HTTPs can transfer data in various formats including HTML, XML, JSON and plain text

    -  http stands for Hper Text Transfer Protocol
    -  https stands for Hper Text Transfer Protocal Secure  
      
      HTTPS is essentially HTTP with security ( The info that is transmitted using https is more secure and integrity of 
      the info is well mainrained in HTTPS )

5) Status Codes in API
      HTTP status codes tell the client whether an API request was successful, failed due to client error, or failed due to server error.

      Classification of status codes
      ------------------------------
            - 1XX ( Informational )
            - 2XX ( Successful )
            - 3XX ( Redirection )
            - 4XX ( Client Error )
            - 5XX ( Server Error )

      Commonly used Status Codes
      --------------------------
            - 200 Ok
            - 201 Created
            - 204 No Content
            - 301 Moved Permanently
            - 400 Bad Request
            - 401 Unauthorized
            - 403 Forbidden
            - 404 Not Found
            - 500 Internal Server Error
            

6) What is Resource, URI and Sub-Resource
      Resource
      --------
            - A Resource is any piece of information that can be named or identified on the web.
            - Can represent any type of object, data or service that can be accessed by clients.
            - A Resource is not just limited to documents or files; it can be anything from a textfile, an image, a ollection 
                  of other resources, a non-virtual object like a person, and even abstract concepts like service.
            - In a social media application, resources could include a user profile, a photo, a list of friends, or even a 
                  specific post or comment.

      URI ( Uniform Resource Identifier )
      ----------------------------------
            - A URI is a string of characters used to identify a resource on the internet either by location, name or both
            - It provides a mechanism for accessing the representation of a resource over the network, typically through specific protocols
                  such as HTTP or HTTPS.
            - URIs are a broad category that include both URLs ( Uniform Resource Locators ) and URNs ( Uniform resource Names ).

      Sub-Resource
      ------------
            - A Sub-Resource is a resource that is hierarchically under another resource.
            - It's a part of a large resource and can be accessed by extending the URI of the parent resource.
            - Sub-resources are often used in RESTful in APIs to maintain a logical hierarchy of data and to facilitate easy access 
                  to related resources.

            EX :-
                  In a blogging platform, you might have a users resource identified by a URI (/users). A specific user could be a resource
                  accessible at /users/{userId}.
                  - If each user can have blog posts, a post would be a sub-resource of that user, identified by something 
                        like /users/{useriId}/posts/{postId}.

7) What is Web Framework ?
      A web framework is a software framework that provides ready-made components and tools to develop web applications quickly, securely and efficiently.
                                          or
      Web Framework is nothing but collection of tools and modules that is needed to do standard tasks across every web application.
                                          or
      A Web Framework is a collection of pre-written code, libraries and tools that helps developers build web applications faster and easier.
       It provides a structure for developing websites, web services and APIs and handles common tasks such as routing, database access, 
       session management and security.
      Why do you need Web Framework ?
            - Websites have a lot in common
            - Security, Databases, URLs, Authentication... more



